WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.193
- So I'm Jessica Seddon.

00:00:09.214 --> 00:00:11.190
I am a senior fellow at
the Yale Jackson School

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:15.630
of Global Affairs, and I am
very happy to have been invited

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:17.670
to chair the last panel of the day,

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.940
which is always kind of a daunting thing

00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.770
on a day that also starts at 8:30

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:26.910
and involves as many
papers and difficult topics

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:31.470
and thoughts and possibilities
as these sessions have.

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.960
I'm also the Jessica who
was invoked in the beginning

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.920
of the session as saying

00:00:36.907 --> 00:00:39.483
that the climate emergency is here now.

00:00:40.487 --> 00:00:41.320
Now I'm not gonna revise that,

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.320
I'm just gonna add a little addendum.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:46.900
I think what's happening when
you start to look at some

00:00:48.633 --> 00:00:50.520
of the growing pile of scientific evidence

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.790
is that we have an unfolding,

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:57.610
likely an accelerating set
of environmental changes

00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:01.980
that include but go way
beyond temperature, rainfall,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.363
and sea level rise.

00:01:05.017 --> 00:01:06.480
These are changes that we have

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.990
an increasingly well grounded,

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:11.670
bounded understanding of.

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.880
We have an understanding of
some of the non-linearities

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:18.630
and also a great sense of
what we don't understand about

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:23.630
how those are going to affect
the infrastructure services.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.710
economic setup that we have.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.930
Ecuador right now is
having rolling blackouts.

00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:30.963
It's a highly hydro
electric dependent economy,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:35.130
and El Nino has not been so
great for hydro electricity.

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:36.570
That's a little bit out of bounds

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.030
from what's happened historically.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:41.040
These are the kinds of things that we can,

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.440
from the science side, anticipate.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.040
At the same time, on the human side,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.093
it's pretty clear that we are
a species that is not passive,

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:54.000
that is entirely capable of responding

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.860
to very significant changes,

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.050
both local, regional, and global.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.321
Sometimes we're self-interested,
often we're banal,

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:02.940
we suffer from moral hazard.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.800
And I think some of the papers
have highlighted frictions

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:10.050
that are deeply social in how
we adapt to these changes.

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:12.900
But we know these things, these
two sides, these two streams

00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:17.900
of knowledge from very large
intellectual enterprises,

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.737
both on the climate science
and climate impacts modeling,

00:02:22.737 --> 00:02:26.130
on the one side and
then also from economics

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.400
and more broadly social
science understanding

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:30.810
of micro foundations.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:34.378
I think we have an enormous amount

00:02:34.378 --> 00:02:36.060
of knowledge about the first principles,

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:38.910
the dynamics of environmental
change on the one side,

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.580
the dynamics of human
responses on the other.

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:45.930
Our challenge going ahead is
how do we put these two streams

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:48.480
together more effectively?

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:52.920
How do we better combine
this understanding

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.520
of the micro to maybe meso foundations

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.710
to build an understanding

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:03.660
and the sense of ability to
make some of the trade-offs

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:05.010
that we need to make now,

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:07.980
use of scarce resources now versus later,

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:11.310
this versus that, here versus there.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:14.370
What are some of the narratives,
the balance, urgency,

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:18.270
but also recognize agency and adaptation

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.830
and anticipating costs.

00:03:19.830 --> 00:03:21.840
Most importantly, and for me,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.860
what are some of the ways
that a combined stream

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.210
of understanding the climate dynamics

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.190
very, very well at high resolution

00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:35.760
and the human social dynamics
at higher resolution,

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:36.630
what does that say?

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:39.090
What does that tell us
about interventions,

00:03:39.090 --> 00:03:42.210
policy instruments,
investments in information,

00:03:42.210 --> 00:03:44.007
investments in credible information?

00:03:44.007 --> 00:03:46.620
The the kinds of
institutions we might need

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.190
to create social tipping points to respond

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:54.190
to the environmental and
climate tipping points

00:03:55.262 --> 00:03:57.300
that are increasingly,

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:02.300
unfortunately, looking
within, looking more probable.

00:04:04.941 --> 00:04:05.924
I think with that,

00:04:05.924 --> 00:04:07.860
that enterprise is both
an intellectual one.

00:04:07.860 --> 00:04:09.360
It's a research agenda,

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:12.160
but it's also in some
ways an institutional one

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.110
in how we organize
interdisciplinary conversations,

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.280
interdisciplinary research agendas

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:22.860
and spaces for framing the questions,

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.060
setting agendas, talking
about how we represent humans

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.270
and climate models and
climate and human models.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:33.210
And for that we have the next hour

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.160
to figure all that stuff out.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.250
But look, luckily we
have an excellent panel.

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:39.390
I'll introduce them in a second

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:41.700
and ask them to come up
for some opening remarks.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.070
But I just wanted to also
say one other thing just

00:04:44.070 --> 00:04:45.510
to set expectations about this,

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:48.930
is this is also a bit of a tortured panel.

00:04:48.930 --> 00:04:53.861
We have an hour and 10 minutes
left, hour and 15 minutes.

00:04:53.861 --> 00:04:56.760
And what I've asked the
panelists to do in kind

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:01.760
