00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:00.720
So what I've been working on,

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:02.860
I'm a macro economist by training

00:09:04.149 --> 00:09:05.700
and have worked on
heterogeneous agent models

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.400
of the macroeconomy of
heterogeneity across households

00:09:09.658 --> 00:09:12.000
and since parlayed that methodological,

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.220
those methodological skills into building

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:15.960
or attempting to build high resolution

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:17.940
integrated assessment models.

00:09:17.940 --> 00:09:21.720
So the idea here is to build
computational laboratories

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:22.830
for evaluating the effects

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:24.420
of climate change in a high degree

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:26.700
of resolution geographically.

00:09:26.700 --> 00:09:29.430
And in particular, what I've
been working on most recently

00:09:29.430 --> 00:09:31.170
is to merge earth system models

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:34.410
with dynamic general
equilibrium economic models

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:36.270
to incorporate in particular weather

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:39.060
and extreme events as well as climate.

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:41.730
So it's called two-Way
coupling the literature.

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:43.440
I think I'm partly on this panel too,

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.344
because I am working with
two climate scientists

00:09:47.344 --> 00:09:49.080
on this coupling that we're working on.

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:51.630
Trudy Sarelmo used to be here at Yale

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:52.463
and that's where I met her.

00:09:52.463 --> 00:09:54.960
And now she's in Norway and
it turns out she's a co-author

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:56.340
with Pierce Forster on part

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:59.426
of the IPCC report I just learned today.

00:09:59.426 --> 00:10:00.270
And then one of her students.

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:01.920
So we're working together trying

00:10:01.920 --> 00:10:04.050
to integrate these two models

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:06.660
and this next point is a
little bit a point more

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:09.270
for climate scientists, but
there's a more economist here

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:10.650
and fewer climate scientists maybe.

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:13.410
But we're trying to build
a fully articulated,

00:10:13.410 --> 00:10:17.130
what we call a structural model
that spells out the behavior

00:10:17.130 --> 00:10:19.620
of the climate system and how
it interacts with the economy

00:10:19.620 --> 00:10:20.850
and how agents behave

00:10:20.850 --> 00:10:23.490
and how they respond to various policies

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:26.313
that might be designed to
a address climate change.

00:10:27.613 --> 00:10:29.880
So we can look at
quantitatively the effects

00:10:29.880 --> 00:10:32.070
of counterfactual policies

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:36.270
and ask questions like, how do
resources move across space?

00:10:36.270 --> 00:10:39.360
How that spatial adaptation
seems critical here,

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.130
who gains and who loses?

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:42.630
And once you have a model like this,

00:10:42.630 --> 00:10:45.300
you can then ask questions about

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:47.610
doing a detailed quantitative analysis

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:49.590
of international climate agreements

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:51.900
or international sharing of climate risk.

00:10:51.900 --> 00:10:53.850
So I'll just show my one picture.

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:54.810
This is just an example.

00:10:54.810 --> 00:10:55.980
This actually looks a lot like

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:57.120
pictures that we've seen today,

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:59.340
although it's coming
from a very different set

00:10:59.340 --> 00:11:01.020
of methodologies and tools.

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:02.880
Without going into details,

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:06.720
this is showing a counterfactual
experiment in a model

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:10.080
calibrates of the global
economy showing who gains

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:13.860
and who lose loses from
a uniform tax on carbon.

00:11:13.860 --> 00:11:16.560
These are percentages
of consumption per year.

00:11:16.560 --> 00:11:18.930
So basically places that are already hot

00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:22.890
are losing, so sorry,
places that are already hot

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:24.480
like a carbon tax,

00:11:24.480 --> 00:11:25.650
they wanna slow down warming

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:29.100
and they're gaining, so that's
just Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:30.390
That's Saharan Africa.

00:11:30.390 --> 00:11:33.810
Russia is actually
losing from a carbon tax

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:36.060
because they're cold and
we like to warm a bit.

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:37.560
So we've actually seen this picture

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:39.780
several times today, I think.

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:42.360
Another thing we can do is
look at inequality, say,

00:11:42.360 --> 00:11:44.370
so we have this, the
model's actually broken

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:46.623
into 19,000 little regions.

00:11:47.664 --> 00:11:48.497
So each,

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:52.130
I'm experiencing what other
people have experienced.

00:11:54.066 --> 00:11:57.077
So each agent is a little, is a region,

00:11:58.200 --> 00:12:00.150
sorry, each agent is a little region.

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:03.780
So 19,000 of these interact
them through markets,

00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:06.690
they make consumption saving
decisions, enter decisions,

00:12:06.690 --> 00:12:08.553
they respond to tax policy.

00:12:09.533 --> 00:12:11.725
They interact through capital markets

00:12:11.725 --> 00:12:14.760
and they interact through
the global climate system.

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:19.680
Alright, so once we have
this model, then I can ask,

00:12:19.680 --> 00:12:21.780
how will inequality change over time?

00:12:21.780 --> 00:12:23.763
So this is showing across,

00:12:24.692 --> 00:12:27.390
this is GDP per capita by country,

00:12:27.390 --> 00:12:29.790
not region, aggregated countries.

00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.320
You can see the 90/10

00:12:31.320 --> 00:12:33.420
percentiles in measure of inequality.

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:35.206
It starts at 30,

00:12:35.206 --> 00:12:37.500
and according to my model,
increases to around 46.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:38.700
That's the top line.

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.950
The second line shows what
happens in an optimal tax.

00:12:40.950 --> 00:12:43.080
And this model actually doesn't
change things very much,

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:44.400
but the point is that you

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:46.560
you can run this
counterfactual experiment.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:50.190
Alright, so what I would say
we need, so getting to my last,

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:51.240
that's sort of where I'm coming in

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:52.620
and what I'm doing,

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:56.490
the challenges that I face or face

00:12:56.490 --> 00:12:58.350
is working across disciplines.

