00:25:02.460 --> 00:25:04.650
let me jump right into it.

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:07.620
I'm going to speak about the macro models,

00:25:07.620 --> 00:25:10.770
especially the integrated
assessment model.

00:25:10.770 --> 00:25:12.360
So some of the first

00:25:12.360 --> 00:25:14.555
of which in fact came from

00:25:14.555 --> 00:25:16.980
the Department of Economics at Yale.

00:25:16.980 --> 00:25:21.676
Of course, I am very
grateful to peers for being

00:25:21.676 --> 00:25:25.353
sufficiently provocative so that I sound,

00:25:26.362 --> 00:25:27.362
I may sound less so.

00:25:29.617 --> 00:25:31.620
The IAMs have become the
dominant modeling tool

00:25:31.620 --> 00:25:33.180
for projecting social economic

00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:36.840
and related energy use
trends into the future.

00:25:36.840 --> 00:25:38.310
The sixth assessment report

00:25:38.310 --> 00:25:42.390
of the IPCC depends quite
heavily on these IAMs.

00:25:42.390 --> 00:25:45.810
While there are a range of
discussions in the IPCC reports

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:48.123
about a lot of other things,

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:52.890
the summary for in the global
model scenarios from the IAMs

00:25:52.890 --> 00:25:56.880
disproportionately dominate
the summary for policy makers,

00:25:56.880 --> 00:25:59.880
which are then further even
more disproportionately picked

00:25:59.880 --> 00:26:02.790
up by the media and
then even more distilled

00:26:02.790 --> 00:26:05.610
and used without the accompanying context

00:26:05.610 --> 00:26:09.903
very often in COP decisions, right?

00:26:11.068 --> 00:26:13.460
So both COP 26 and COP 27,
for example, quote the number

00:26:13.460 --> 00:26:16.710
of 43% reductions in GE emissions,

00:26:16.710 --> 00:26:18.340
which corresponds to the median

00:26:19.174 --> 00:26:21.690
of all global scenarios
assessed by the IPCC

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:23.973
for the 1.5 degree cells here target.

00:26:25.739 --> 00:26:28.474
So it's become a prescription for policy

00:26:28.474 --> 00:26:30.143
and so it becomes
important to analyze this.

00:26:31.117 --> 00:26:32.550
My work focuses on questions of equity

00:26:32.550 --> 00:26:35.243
and justice in modeling
burden sharing models

00:26:35.243 --> 00:26:37.053
as well as other kinds of models.

00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:42.093
So I'm not going to go into the details

00:26:42.093 --> 00:26:43.200
of what the IAMs are.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:46.970
I'm speaking to an audience
that is probably more aware

00:26:46.970 --> 00:26:49.593
of what these do than I am.

00:26:52.027 --> 00:26:54.730
But what we have
essentially is a combination

00:26:59.031 --> 00:27:01.860
of macroeconomic models that are based on

00:27:01.860 --> 00:27:03.780
neoclassical frameworks,

00:27:03.780 --> 00:27:05.940
energy models that focus on minimization

00:27:05.940 --> 00:27:08.190
of costs of abatement,

00:27:08.190 --> 00:27:12.700
vegetation models with that
system feedbacks coupled

00:27:13.551 --> 00:27:16.890
with scenario frameworks that
are essentially socioeconomic

00:27:16.890 --> 00:27:18.390
storylines for the future.

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:19.293
So you have,

00:27:20.736 --> 00:27:22.890
will there be sustainable development,

00:27:22.890 --> 00:27:24.630
will there be fossil fuel dependence?

00:27:24.630 --> 00:27:27.540
Will there be cooperation
or regional rivalry?

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:28.800
These are the kinds of storylines

00:27:28.800 --> 00:27:30.633
that are projected into the future.

00:27:32.856 --> 00:27:35.140
But I think one of the first issues

00:27:36.803 --> 00:27:38.610
is that the world in
these models is divided

00:27:38.610 --> 00:27:40.110
by geographical regions.

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:43.680
And this is what we saw
throughout the day as well when

00:27:43.680 --> 00:27:45.390
modeling results were presented.

00:27:45.390 --> 00:27:46.920
Which itself is a problem I think

00:27:46.920 --> 00:27:50.910
because Gina spoke a little
bit about the problem of risks

00:27:50.910 --> 00:27:52.980
and how they're assessed
when this happens.

00:27:52.980 --> 00:27:57.090
But such a distribution doesn't
tell us about differentiated

00:27:57.090 --> 00:28:00.123
levels of development,
production, consumption, etcetera.

00:28:01.020 --> 00:28:02.910
When there is an economic differentiation

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:04.113
is only for the OECD.

00:28:04.974 --> 00:28:06.840
So it's only the OECD that are economies.

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:10.770
The rest of us are
essentially just geographies.

00:28:10.770 --> 00:28:12.840
And so that is one issue.

00:28:12.840 --> 00:28:15.540
The second is of course, that
irrespective of storylines

00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:16.563
that you construct,

00:28:18.373 --> 00:28:20.430
the macroeconomic models that essentially

00:28:20.430 --> 00:28:22.150
pursue PTO optimality

00:28:25.085 --> 00:28:26.580
and most of the macroeconomic models

00:28:26.580 --> 00:28:28.380
that follow the structure

00:28:28.380 --> 00:28:31.260
and the frameworks that are used,

00:28:31.260 --> 00:28:34.350
freeze income inequalities
at current levels,

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:37.170
not allowing any more conversions than is

00:28:37.170 --> 00:28:40.440
considered reasonable
based on historical trends.

00:28:40.440 --> 00:28:42.183
So you have inequalities in,

00:28:43.080 --> 00:28:44.370
these are for results

00:28:44.370 --> 00:28:47.610
of the IPCC scenarios
that we have assessed.

00:28:47.610 --> 00:28:50.700
About 556 scenarios corresponding

00:28:50.700 --> 00:28:53.850
to the 1.5 and two degrees
Celsius temperature targets.

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:56.340
So you have any inequalities

00:28:56.340 --> 00:29:01.173
that persist across regions even by 2050.

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:05.370
These GDP and consumption figures

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:09.150
are, of course, linked quite
significantly with energy.

00:29:09.150 --> 00:29:11.910
And so you have energy poverty

00:29:11.910 --> 00:29:15.660
and energy inequalities that
persist also throughout.

00:29:15.660 --> 00:29:19.050
And this is tied to how energy
demand and supply are modeled

00:29:19.050 --> 00:29:21.270
and coupled with the macroeconomic models.

00:29:21.270 --> 00:29:24.690
The objective is to minimize
the cost of mitigation.

00:29:24.690 --> 00:29:27.450
So the solution is to
find optimality in terms

00:29:27.450 --> 00:29:30.153
that are both spatially
and temporarily cheaper.

00:29:31.936 --> 00:29:33.900
So if it is cheaper to reduce emissions

00:29:33.900 --> 00:29:37.380
to agricultural and forestry
in the developing countries

00:29:37.380 --> 00:29:39.690
as compared to say the
reduction of oil, gas,

00:29:39.690 --> 00:29:41.970
and coal use in developed countries,

00:29:41.970 --> 00:29:43.170
that will be the solution.

00:29:43.170 --> 00:29:47.430
And so you have extreme
levels of energy equality,

00:29:47.430 --> 00:29:50.733
persisting across model
scenarios even in the future.

00:29:52.541 --> 00:29:54.960
And the models therefore then
end up projecting much higher

00:29:54.960 --> 00:29:56.760
levels of mitigation burdens,

00:29:56.760 --> 00:29:59.790
both in the short and the long
term on developing countries.

00:29:59.790 --> 00:30:02.790
On an average, for example,
Sub-Saharan Africa is expected

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:05.683
to reduce emissions by 80% in this decade

00:30:05.683 --> 00:30:07.433
across the scenarios.

00:30:07.433 --> 00:30:10.680
Whereas the reductions
are about 50% for Europe

00:30:10.680 --> 00:30:12.090
and North America,

00:30:12.090 --> 00:30:14.490
in line with the NDCs of
these countries, right?

00:30:15.540 --> 00:30:17.610
The models also project large amounts

00:30:17.610 --> 00:30:20.340
of carbon dioxide removal across models.

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:25.340
Most of this is coming from the
global south through energy,

00:30:25.350 --> 00:30:28.920
the diversion of land from food crops

00:30:28.920 --> 00:30:32.190
to energy crops increasing
the risk of hunger

00:30:32.190 --> 00:30:34.652
across the regions.

00:30:34.652 --> 00:30:38.640
And so therefore the current
trend of decreasing hunger,

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:41.430
decreasing risk of hunger
is in fact reversed in some

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:45.150
of the more extreme
scenarios that are assessed

00:30:45.150 --> 00:30:47.640
by the IPCC in the future.

00:30:47.640 --> 00:30:49.923
So the questions of equity questions,

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:55.770
of dealing with questions
of vulnerability and risk,

00:30:55.770 --> 00:30:57.600
which are unequally distributed today

00:30:57.600 --> 00:31:00.813
and remain unequally
distributed even in the future,

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:06.360
are not addressed with the
current modeling framework.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:09.360
The global south is denied
the means to build resilience

00:31:09.360 --> 00:31:11.520
that we've been speaking
about throughout the day

00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:14.880
and this is a major gap, but
there are ways to address this

00:31:14.880 --> 00:31:16.530
and maybe we come back to this state.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:17.980
So I'll stop here, thank you.

