00:43:15.780 --> 00:43:18.333
but in some ways if we can,

00:43:20.250 --> 00:43:22.050
there's a willingness, there's a goodwill,

00:43:22.050 --> 00:43:25.077
there's potentially younger scientists

00:43:25.077 --> 00:43:27.527
who wanna change the world,
but then there's also

00:43:29.140 --> 00:43:31.270
where there's a pull, there's a response

00:43:32.905 --> 00:43:34.230
and that research then generates

00:43:34.230 --> 00:43:37.383
or potentially could
start a positive cycle.

00:43:38.850 --> 00:43:41.190
So that leads us, I think
into a second set of questions

00:43:41.190 --> 00:43:44.970
that will come to and how
do we articulate that pull

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:47.100
and respond to it effectively knowing

00:43:47.100 --> 00:43:49.807
that there are frictions
for doing so in the way that

00:43:49.807 --> 00:43:52.230
sciences are structured.

00:43:52.230 --> 00:43:56.730
I wanna turn it back to you
Tejal, for just your thoughts on

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:59.820
rewards and how to increase
them for the kind of work

00:43:59.820 --> 00:44:03.400
that excavates the assumptions
that you've been looking at

00:44:05.520 --> 00:44:07.830
and yeah, your thoughts on
what might make it easier

00:44:07.830 --> 00:44:09.093
to reduce the frictions.

00:44:11.640 --> 00:44:15.420
- Yeah, I think, I mean
I agree with the peers

00:44:16.258 --> 00:44:18.186
to the extent that

00:44:18.186 --> 00:44:22.350
there is definitely a difference
in how younger people.

00:44:22.350 --> 00:44:25.050
Now my students for example, I have a mix

00:44:25.050 --> 00:44:28.380
of social scientists and
engineers as students

00:44:28.380 --> 00:44:30.280
and they work together and collaborate

00:44:31.557 --> 00:44:33.600
and produce work together

00:44:33.600 --> 00:44:37.770
because the environment
in which we do science

00:44:37.770 --> 00:44:39.750
has changed to a certain extent.

00:44:39.750 --> 00:44:41.040
And the problem of climate change,

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:44.370
I think everybody is recognized doesn't,

00:44:44.370 --> 00:44:45.960
respect disciplinary boundaries.

00:44:45.960 --> 00:44:49.800
You need to speak across disciplines.

00:44:49.800 --> 00:44:52.210
But I think another issue is that

00:44:54.007 --> 00:44:57.630
while IPCC is not just siloed
in terms of disciplines,

00:44:57.630 --> 00:45:00.363
but also in terms of
where authors come from.

00:45:01.198 --> 00:45:02.220
I mean especially if you look at the IAMs

00:45:02.220 --> 00:45:06.000
and the scenario modeling, it's
largely based in the global

00:45:06.000 --> 00:45:08.070
north and that's a,

00:45:08.070 --> 00:45:10.080
if you talk about global climate policy,

00:45:10.080 --> 00:45:13.137
that's a huge gap because you have 82%

00:45:13.137 --> 00:45:15.030
of the global population that's very,

00:45:15.030 --> 00:45:19.932
very invested in climate
policy making and negotiations,

00:45:19.932 --> 00:45:23.880
but plays very little role in introducing

00:45:23.880 --> 00:45:28.511
and assessing the science,
be it the natural science,

00:45:28.511 --> 00:45:29.661
or the physical science

00:45:31.596 --> 00:45:35.003
or the social science that
goes into the IPCC reports.

00:45:35.003 --> 00:45:37.360
And that perhaps needs to change also

00:45:38.772 --> 00:45:40.222
as conversations are changing

00:45:41.120 --> 00:45:44.430
with the newer generations
maybe doing things differently.

00:45:44.430 --> 00:45:48.363
- So just one, I mean I think
that that raises a really,

00:45:49.373 --> 00:45:50.940
that is a good segue
into a really important

00:45:50.940 --> 00:45:52.170
second set of questions.

00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:55.630
Sorry, I'm human so I can't speak to you

00:45:56.898 --> 00:45:57.731
and have Tejal's face behind me,

00:45:57.731 --> 00:46:00.663
which is why I have
this awkward pose here.

00:46:01.890 --> 00:46:04.650
But I think that segues into
the second set of questions,

00:46:04.650 --> 00:46:08.460
which is really how do we create

00:46:08.460 --> 00:46:11.740
and articulate not only a demand

00:46:14.087 --> 00:46:16.860
for the kind of integrated
research to inform questions

00:46:16.860 --> 00:46:19.890
that are very present here and now,

00:46:19.890 --> 00:46:23.730
but also some degree of discernment

00:46:23.730 --> 00:46:27.273
and willingness for not everybody,

00:46:29.107 --> 00:46:31.320
but at least somebody

00:46:31.320 --> 00:46:33.678
to look into the assumptions

00:46:33.678 --> 00:46:37.230
in ever more complicated models,

00:46:37.230 --> 00:46:39.933
either climate or human or both.

00:46:41.906 --> 00:46:43.756
And to highlight the problematic ones

00:46:44.828 --> 00:46:47.580
because I think that
articulation of demand

00:46:47.580 --> 00:46:51.580
as well as building of discernment

00:46:53.425 --> 00:46:56.130
are two critical features of creating

00:46:56.130 --> 00:46:58.590
an environment that might be sufficient

00:46:58.590 --> 00:47:00.660
to push us over the frictions,

00:47:00.660 --> 00:47:03.750
to generating a useful set of knowledge.

00:47:03.750 --> 00:47:05.733
Tejal do you have any ideas on that?

00:47:07.048 --> 00:47:08.640
What might just increase the discernment

00:47:08.640 --> 00:47:11.520
and draw more attention
to these assumptions,

00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:12.920
these and other assumptions?

00:47:17.940 --> 00:47:22.200
- I don't know, that's
a bit of a difficult

00:47:22.200 --> 00:47:24.033
question in the sense that a lot of,

00:47:25.249 --> 00:47:26.550
it depends on what assumptions
we are talking about.

00:47:26.550 --> 00:47:29.763
For example, in the climate science,

00:47:32.010 --> 00:47:32.843
domain,

00:47:34.050 --> 00:47:37.380
you deal with a lot of known unknowns

00:47:37.380 --> 00:47:38.973
and a lot of unknown unknowns.

00:47:41.305 --> 00:47:43.093
And as our understanding

00:47:43.093 --> 00:47:44.940
of the natural world expands,

00:47:44.940 --> 00:47:48.090
our computational capacities increase.

00:47:48.090 --> 00:47:50.850
We perhaps are able to answer questions

00:47:50.850 --> 00:47:52.700
and look at assumptions more closely.

00:47:54.111 --> 00:47:55.411
But in the social sciences

00:47:56.246 --> 00:47:58.313
and especially when you couple these

00:47:58.313 --> 00:48:00.563
with the natural sciences as happens

00:48:00.563 --> 00:48:01.713
in the modeling domain,

00:48:05.803 --> 00:48:08.280
a lot of the frictions
are ideological perhaps

00:48:08.280 --> 00:48:10.200
and also based on perspectives in terms

00:48:10.200 --> 00:48:12.120
of where we come from
and the kind of questions

00:48:12.120 --> 00:48:13.370
that we're interested in.

00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:19.410
Modeling in the global north
interested in answering

00:48:19.410 --> 00:48:20.560
questions of equity

00:48:22.170 --> 00:48:24.210
or is it something that only

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:26.691
researchers in the
global south will raise?

00:48:26.691 --> 00:48:28.600
And if the raise, will there be a,

00:48:29.989 --> 00:48:32.100
will there be capacity to respond

00:48:32.100 --> 00:48:34.563
to that demand in the global south?

00:48:35.612 --> 00:48:37.680
So will we be able to
build models for example,

00:48:37.680 --> 00:48:41.520
that don't trade off equity
against efficiency, right?

00:48:41.520 --> 00:48:44.370
That don't start with the objective

00:48:44.370 --> 00:48:45.810
function of minimizing cost,

00:48:45.810 --> 00:48:47.760
but perhaps start with
the objective function

00:48:47.760 --> 00:48:49.503
of maximizing resilience.

00:48:51.395 --> 00:48:53.040
So do we have the capacity to respond

00:48:53.040 --> 00:48:55.860
to that demand even though
there is a political demand

00:48:55.860 --> 00:48:58.377
to address those kinds of questions?

00:48:58.377 --> 00:49:00.713
So that I think still remains open.

00:49:00.713 --> 00:49:01.920
So it's not something

00:49:01.920 --> 00:49:04.050
that you can answer perhaps
in a uniform fashion

00:49:04.050 --> 00:49:05.553
across domains in my opinion.

00:49:07.012 --> 00:49:09.450
- So I wanna ask you next
Regina for your thoughts.

00:49:09.450 --> 00:49:11.490
I mean you showed a nice graph

00:49:11.490 --> 00:49:12.990
that's clearly in the public domain

00:49:12.990 --> 00:49:16.771
and it's in the IPCC report of where,

00:49:16.771 --> 00:49:21.000
what level of certainty
we have about relevant

00:49:21.000 --> 00:49:24.720
climatic conditions in
different areas of the world

00:49:24.720 --> 00:49:26.790
and made the point that underneath

00:49:26.790 --> 00:49:31.790
some of the more general
predictions and statements,

00:49:32.040 --> 00:49:34.560
there is a huge geographic variation

00:49:34.560 --> 00:49:36.870
in the origins of that information.

00:49:36.870 --> 00:49:38.010
That's something that's out there,

00:49:38.010 --> 00:49:41.010
but it's not widely known or recognized.

00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:43.170
Do you see a space

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:46.750
or a way to create more discernment

00:49:47.856 --> 00:49:50.853
and demand for evening that out?

00:49:56.340 --> 00:50:00.240
- Yes, so what we are trying to develop

00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:04.920
in the world climb research
program now is hubs,

00:50:05.880 --> 00:50:08.010
local hubs that they,

00:50:08.010 --> 00:50:10.600
and we can help them to develop

00:50:12.843 --> 00:50:14.520
the information that is available.

00:50:14.520 --> 00:50:16.170
I'll give an example 'cause

00:50:16.170 --> 00:50:18.750
otherwise, so the MET office

00:50:18.750 --> 00:50:21.250
of Brazil doesn't do a good job

00:50:22.338 --> 00:50:24.240
of delivering a seasonal prediction,

00:50:24.240 --> 00:50:25.290
which is really important

00:50:25.290 --> 00:50:27.360
for agriculture, energy.

00:50:27.360 --> 00:50:30.570
Brazil is hydroelectric 65%.

00:50:30.570 --> 00:50:33.513
So that's really important
for energy purpose too.

00:50:36.081 --> 00:50:38.065
And I live in a southern Brazil

00:50:38.065 --> 00:50:40.733
and certainly the
predictions from the METOFs

00:50:41.637 --> 00:50:45.300
for the whole Brazil and
southern Brazil doesn't work.

00:50:45.300 --> 00:50:47.643
The global predictions from you know,

00:50:48.964 --> 00:50:50.763
other places also doesn't work.

00:50:51.979 --> 00:50:55.020
You need a lot of investment
in capacity building

00:50:55.020 --> 00:50:58.863
and money to run these models,
which is not available.

00:50:59.748 --> 00:51:02.943
So what we do is we get
the local state government,

00:51:04.064 --> 00:51:06.420
a small group of meteorologists

00:51:06.420 --> 00:51:09.990
and we help them develop simple tools

00:51:09.990 --> 00:51:12.330
that they can get this information from,

00:51:12.330 --> 00:51:14.820
say emergency response.

00:51:14.820 --> 00:51:18.940
My state has a lot of extreme events

00:51:20.400 --> 00:51:21.813
and then we are doing this,

00:51:22.651 --> 00:51:25.260
we getting these hubs around the world,

00:51:25.260 --> 00:51:26.700
particularly in the global cell

00:51:26.700 --> 00:51:30.210
and help them with really simple tools

00:51:30.210 --> 00:51:32.343
that doesn't require too much money.

00:51:33.741 --> 00:51:36.660
We have this information
already enough information

00:51:36.660 --> 00:51:40.830
available to make them more
resilient and then they learn

00:51:40.830 --> 00:51:44.850
and they can be self-sufficient
to start to kind

00:51:44.850 --> 00:51:47.730
of producing that climate
information that they need

00:51:47.730 --> 00:51:52.730
for say whatever, emergency responsible

00:51:53.280 --> 00:51:55.650
extreme events or I dunno,

00:51:55.650 --> 00:51:58.470
energy, etcetera,

00:51:58.470 --> 00:51:59.373
policies.

00:52:02.958 --> 00:52:04.310
- Just gonna steal this
back for one second.

