00:58:12.090 --> 00:58:13.770
we get a hurricane locally.

00:58:13.770 --> 00:58:17.490
So we need to model those
somewhat more local impacts

00:58:17.490 --> 00:58:20.170
and then study how we
can share those risks

00:58:21.405 --> 00:58:23.550
across governments or countries.

00:58:23.550 --> 00:58:26.070
So I think it's quite important
to bring in inequality

00:58:26.070 --> 00:58:27.720
that there's heterogeneity impacts.

00:58:27.720 --> 00:58:30.837
I think that's pretty
well established by now,

00:58:30.837 --> 00:58:32.010
but when I first did my
first version of the model,

00:58:32.010 --> 00:58:35.280
like this is crazy how it is
coming out so heterogeneous

00:58:35.280 --> 00:58:36.960
and I think weather is
just super important.

00:58:36.960 --> 00:58:40.080
There's some work I think
Rossi Hanberg and Bilal,

00:58:40.080 --> 00:58:42.840
the paper that Sam
mentioned earlier is doing

00:58:42.840 --> 00:58:45.458
some of that, but we need
to do much more of that.

00:58:45.458 --> 00:58:46.445
I would think.

00:58:46.445 --> 00:58:47.370
And that requires bringing in the richness

00:58:47.370 --> 00:58:49.866
of earth system models or maybe using

00:58:49.866 --> 00:58:51.120
a very good emulator

00:58:51.120 --> 00:58:54.243
that will let us look at
regional weather impacts.

00:59:01.050 --> 00:59:03.690
- So I'm not sure if what I'm
about to say is a question

00:59:03.690 --> 00:59:07.050
or a comment, but I felt
like this was so pessimistic

00:59:07.050 --> 00:59:10.140
compared to well having,

00:59:10.140 --> 00:59:12.780
spent nearly two years in the
White House science office

00:59:12.780 --> 00:59:15.630
and looking at how science
was coming into policy.

00:59:15.630 --> 00:59:18.570
I came away from that
experience being completely odd,

00:59:18.570 --> 00:59:22.020
at least how much science
we were trying to gobble up

00:59:22.020 --> 00:59:25.863
and organize and repackage
and how important it was.

00:59:26.760 --> 00:59:30.180
And what I took away was
that there's a missing link

00:59:30.180 --> 00:59:34.200
in the feedback scientists
get when that happens.

00:59:34.200 --> 00:59:35.880
And I think this is part of the challenge

00:59:35.880 --> 00:59:38.370
is it's, I think we as a
scientific community have

00:59:38.370 --> 00:59:39.960
to also think about like what feedbacks

00:59:39.960 --> 00:59:41.760
are we actually expecting?

00:59:41.760 --> 00:59:44.993
Because it seemed to me when
I was at the White House,

00:59:44.993 --> 00:59:46.830
like the most important work
was like, you don't cite it,

00:59:46.830 --> 00:59:48.540
you let the secretary take credit for it

00:59:48.540 --> 00:59:49.380
or you say,

00:59:49.380 --> 00:59:52.180
you let somebody else take
it's plagiarism was actually,

00:59:53.470 --> 00:59:54.303
I don't wanna accuse the
White House of plagiarism,

00:59:54.303 --> 00:59:57.270
but like just poaching stuff was like,

00:59:57.270 --> 00:59:58.530
that's the really important stuff.

00:59:58.530 --> 01:00:00.360
When you start studying
it's less important.

01:00:00.360 --> 01:00:02.580
And so I'm wondering
just what do we expect

01:00:02.580 --> 01:00:05.498
as a scientific community in a world where

01:00:05.498 --> 01:00:08.913
we're super unique as researchers.

01:00:10.006 --> 01:00:11.280
Like people read our
papers, they cite them,

01:00:11.280 --> 01:00:12.540
this is how we evaluate stuff.

01:00:12.540 --> 01:00:15.480
We look at like exactly we count.

01:00:15.480 --> 01:00:16.920
But even if you go to
like a corporate world,

01:00:16.920 --> 01:00:19.830
nobody knows exactly
anybody's individual impact.

01:00:19.830 --> 01:00:23.280
And so how we're operating
with a world in policy

01:00:23.280 --> 01:00:25.860
that like individual
impacts aren't tracked

01:00:25.860 --> 01:00:28.740
and so what are our
expectations for being able

01:00:28.740 --> 01:00:32.100
to think about how our sciences
is responsive to policy

01:00:32.100 --> 01:00:33.270
and how we understand that

01:00:33.270 --> 01:00:34.970
and how we get those signals back.

01:00:38.760 --> 01:00:40.230
- Does anyone have any thoughts on that?

01:00:40.230 --> 01:00:42.153
I think it's an excellent question.

01:00:43.230 --> 01:00:47.643
- And I would say that I've had
exactly the same experience,

01:00:48.512 --> 01:00:51.600
that yeah, I do see lots
of examples in my career

01:00:51.600 --> 01:00:53.350
where it's really worked very well

01:00:54.883 --> 01:00:58.063
and that we should take
optimism from that though.

01:01:01.041 --> 01:01:02.467
But I've also through the IPCC,

01:01:02.467 --> 01:01:04.390
I work in other countries a lot

01:01:06.585 --> 01:01:07.585
and I think in a lot

01:01:10.137 --> 01:01:13.253
of countries they don't have
the same capacity there.

01:01:14.816 --> 01:01:16.360
And so perhaps that is really

01:01:18.318 --> 01:01:19.947
something that kind of tell

01:01:19.947 --> 01:01:22.020
and kind of Regina could comment on.

01:01:22.020 --> 01:01:25.470
You said you came from a
particularly bad kind part

01:01:25.470 --> 01:01:29.013
of in Brazil where they
completely ignored you.

01:01:34.050 --> 01:01:37.263
- Well lemme just get my thoughts here.

01:01:39.240 --> 01:01:42.330
The thing is that I think
we have a lot of information

01:01:42.330 --> 01:01:43.800
for mitigation purpose.

01:01:43.800 --> 01:01:48.800
What makes me like, I think
the frustrated is that

01:01:50.308 --> 01:01:51.660
we know what we have to do,

01:01:51.660 --> 01:01:53.550
but politically in the high levels,

01:01:53.550 --> 01:01:55.410
we are not having the engagement.

01:01:55.410 --> 01:01:57.100
This is very upsetting

01:01:58.182 --> 01:02:01.020
and I'm not optimistic when
I look into the mitigation

01:02:01.020 --> 01:02:04.470
aspect and I know that we
already have enough information

01:02:04.470 --> 01:02:09.300
that we needed to get
to net zero, etcetera.

01:02:09.300 --> 01:02:11.540
But what give me hope is when I look

01:02:11.540 --> 01:02:14.520
to the bottom up approach that I say,

01:02:14.520 --> 01:02:19.469
which is more this
grassroots process of empower

01:02:19.469 --> 01:02:24.469
the local community to make
sense, then we can build trust.

01:02:24.480 --> 01:02:28.470
We can use that conversation
in a local level,

01:02:28.470 --> 01:02:32.280
but it's not like for scientists,
that's sometimes it's not

01:02:32.280 --> 01:02:34.833
so glorious as the high level.

01:02:36.196 --> 01:02:38.946
Scientists wanna get up there,
they wanna just talk to,

01:02:40.792 --> 01:02:43.468
and make a difference with politics

01:02:43.468 --> 01:02:44.468
and in a high level.

01:02:45.868 --> 01:02:46.868
And it's not like a,

01:02:48.615 --> 01:02:52.787
but I see hope in the grassroots
science developing this.

01:02:54.364 --> 01:02:56.763
So I dunno if I passed this pessimistic,

01:02:59.035 --> 01:03:02.266
but when I think about the
bottom up approach in terms

01:03:02.266 --> 01:03:04.860
of climate science with communities,

01:03:04.860 --> 01:03:06.870
then I think we will change.

01:03:06.870 --> 01:03:08.790
'Cause in this conversation
it can change even

01:03:08.790 --> 01:03:10.830
how they vote, you know?

01:03:10.830 --> 01:03:14.550
And that's what think gonna
make much more impact than try

01:03:14.550 --> 01:03:17.193
to talk to the politics
and change in their mind.

01:03:20.730 --> 01:03:21.630
- Tejal, did you have anything

01:03:21.630 --> 01:03:22.680
to add on this point?

01:03:26.340 --> 01:03:27.190
- Can't hear you.

01:03:28.260 --> 01:03:31.560
- Apologies, can you hear me now?

01:03:31.560 --> 01:03:35.100
- Now we can hear you.
- Yeah. Alright.

01:03:35.100 --> 01:03:39.423
No, so just briefly,
I mean I'm sorry if I,

01:03:40.915 --> 01:03:44.043
I contributed to the
pessimism of the message,

01:03:45.330 --> 01:03:46.580
but I don't think it's a,

01:03:47.430 --> 01:03:48.483
there is a problem.

01:03:49.443 --> 01:03:53.370
In fact in a lot of developing
countries you have far more

01:03:53.370 --> 01:03:54.960
ready uptake of the signs.

01:03:54.960 --> 01:03:58.560
You have far less climate
denial in developing countries

01:03:58.560 --> 01:04:00.600
irrespective of capacities,

01:04:00.600 --> 01:04:04.110
perhaps scientific in-house
scientific capacities.

01:04:04.110 --> 01:04:06.480
There has been much more adoption.

01:04:06.480 --> 01:04:10.020
Clearly of climate policies across regions

01:04:10.020 --> 01:04:11.370
in the developing world.

01:04:11.370 --> 01:04:13.113
In India for example, you have,

01:04:14.190 --> 01:04:17.250
agricultural departments and
newer crop models coming up

01:04:17.250 --> 01:04:20.020
and people working at regional

01:04:22.596 --> 01:04:24.990
and national level models as well

01:04:24.990 --> 01:04:29.583
as actually working on the
field trying to experiment.

01:04:30.851 --> 01:04:31.684
And this is a change remarkably

01:04:31.684 --> 01:04:33.093
in a very short span of time.

01:04:34.170 --> 01:04:38.130
Given that we work in academic time spans,

01:04:38.130 --> 01:04:39.003
we don't think,

01:04:40.860 --> 01:04:43.500
there's that much of an
impact and I agree that some

01:04:43.500 --> 01:04:46.050
of this cannot be measured
in terms of publications

01:04:46.050 --> 01:04:49.680
and citations and it shouldn't be.

01:04:49.680 --> 01:04:53.400
But unfortunately we are also
slaves to our professions

01:04:53.400 --> 01:04:55.740
and that's what gets
us tenure, for example.

01:04:55.740 --> 01:04:57.030
So you need to publish and that's

01:04:57.030 --> 01:04:59.823
how we end up thinking
about measuring our impact.

01:05:02.043 --> 01:05:03.184
But it is true that there is,

01:05:03.184 --> 01:05:05.580
there has been significant
shifts in the way in which people

01:05:05.580 --> 01:05:09.540
are thinking about the
environment and climate,

01:05:09.540 --> 01:05:10.950
even in developing countries

01:05:10.950 --> 01:05:14.700
where you may not have as much
of a footprint of the science

01:05:14.700 --> 01:05:17.703
as you do in developed countries.

01:05:19.847 --> 01:05:22.090
But I think the question for example

01:05:23.319 --> 01:05:26.793
of what constitutes the a correct
interpretation of science,

01:05:26.793 --> 01:05:29.310
where this entire focus on net zero

01:05:29.310 --> 01:05:33.270
in the developed countries
is only a partial adoption

01:05:33.270 --> 01:05:34.800
of what the real science is.

01:05:34.800 --> 01:05:36.480
Nobody's talking about carbon budgets.

01:05:36.480 --> 01:05:40.350
In fact, the UK that spoke
about carbon budgets,

01:05:40.350 --> 01:05:44.043
which is in fact the more
complete measure of warming,

01:05:47.010 --> 01:05:48.900
it spoke about it in its earlier plans,

01:05:48.900 --> 01:05:52.323
but has now moved away from
it in pursuit of net zero.

01:05:53.777 --> 01:05:57.630
So you have, sometimes
even science feeding into,

01:05:57.630 --> 01:06:01.173
it's not just science
providing evidence for policy,

01:06:02.460 --> 01:06:05.070
but policy makers telling scientists

01:06:05.070 --> 01:06:06.330
what they would like to hear,

01:06:06.330 --> 01:06:08.460
what it is convenient for them to hear.

01:06:08.460 --> 01:06:10.860
And I think this goes back to
a little bit of the question

01:06:10.860 --> 01:06:15.390
that Jessica had about
the pull and push, right?

01:06:15.390 --> 01:06:19.300
Is it always the case
that you have scientists

01:06:20.164 --> 01:06:21.660
who are studying this

01:06:21.660 --> 01:06:25.080
with all the uncertainties
involved telling policy makers

01:06:25.080 --> 01:06:27.540
this is what we have and
this is what is happening.

01:06:27.540 --> 01:06:32.115
Or do you also have now situation
where policy makers know

01:06:32.115 --> 01:06:33.720
to a certain extent
what the challenges are

01:06:33.720 --> 01:06:35.760
and what the issues are,

01:06:35.760 --> 01:06:39.510
but they also ha know that
there's inertia in the system

01:06:39.510 --> 01:06:41.310
and they're giving a
message to the scientists

01:06:41.310 --> 01:06:44.071
that this much is convenient to us.

01:06:44.071 --> 01:06:47.403
So don't amplify the
scientific message beyond that.

01:06:48.884 --> 01:06:49.860
And I am a little fearful that this is

01:06:49.860 --> 01:06:52.230
what is happening that you have,

01:06:52.230 --> 01:06:53.650
we are past the era

01:06:53.650 --> 01:06:58.080
where scientists were
pushing for certain policies

01:06:58.080 --> 01:06:59.013
and you have,

01:06:59.940 --> 01:07:02.580
you have now scientists being dictated to,

01:07:02.580 --> 01:07:03.840
in terms of what kind

01:07:03.840 --> 01:07:06.180
of science to produce and that's becoming

01:07:06.180 --> 01:07:07.230
a bit of a problem.

01:07:07.230 --> 01:07:10.307
So we perhaps need to reverse the trend

01:07:10.307 --> 01:07:12.210
and push once again.

01:07:12.210 --> 01:07:13.830
Go back to an era where we needed

01:07:13.830 --> 01:07:17.460
to push maybe reality of approaches,

01:07:17.460 --> 01:07:21.450
welcome heterogeneity in
terms of different approaches

01:07:21.450 --> 01:07:23.730
and perspectives in terms
of how we do the modeling.

01:07:23.730 --> 01:07:24.690
Thank you.

01:07:24.690 --> 01:07:26.910
- So this has been a.
- Go ahead.

01:07:26.910 --> 01:07:28.577
- I just wanted to add a,

01:07:28.577 --> 01:07:29.410
maybe a more optimistic point,

01:07:29.410 --> 01:07:31.801
but first I'll start with a nor a story

01:07:31.801 --> 01:07:33.600
about Nordhaus', pessimistic,
very first version,

01:07:33.600 --> 01:07:34.960
if you read his biography

01:07:35.880 --> 01:07:37.800
that he put up for the Nobel Prize.

01:07:37.800 --> 01:07:38.760
The very first version

01:07:38.760 --> 01:07:40.680
of his very first climate
model was rejected

01:07:40.680 --> 01:07:41.910
by all the economics journals.

01:07:41.910 --> 01:07:43.680
He had to publish it in science.

01:07:43.680 --> 01:07:46.860
But I think that's, this is
where I'll switch to optimistic.

01:07:46.860 --> 01:07:48.570
I think within economics there's been,

01:07:48.570 --> 01:07:51.090
I think an explosion of research
on climate since I started

01:07:51.090 --> 01:07:53.280
working on this 10 years ago or something.

01:07:53.280 --> 01:07:54.780
I think the pull factor,

01:07:54.780 --> 01:07:57.950
there might be partly just climate change.

01:07:57.950 --> 01:08:00.160
We can see it really happening.

01:08:00.160 --> 01:08:00.993
It's like it seems really important

01:08:00.993 --> 01:08:02.670
and maybe it didn't seem before.

01:08:02.670 --> 01:08:05.250
Also, I think environmental
economics is viewed

01:08:05.250 --> 01:08:07.950
as more methodologically interesting

01:08:07.950 --> 01:08:08.880
than maybe it was before,

01:08:08.880 --> 01:08:10.890
maybe it was unfair to
environmental economics,

01:08:10.890 --> 01:08:13.770
but be seeing like you can
use all these modern tools

01:08:13.770 --> 01:08:16.320
from other IO or from
Micro and bring them in,

01:08:16.320 --> 01:08:17.153
bring 'em here.

01:08:17.153 --> 01:08:19.200
But where I'm a little more
pessimistic is whether you can

01:08:19.200 --> 01:08:21.900
convince economists to get

01:08:21.900 --> 01:08:23.730
actively involved in the policy world.

01:08:23.730 --> 01:08:26.397
'Cause we don't reward that for tenure.

01:08:26.397 --> 01:08:28.920
And then working across the,
so economists are maybe happy

01:08:28.920 --> 01:08:32.190
with their models, but
then I find skepticism from

01:08:32.190 --> 01:08:34.530
sociologists or maybe climate

01:08:34.530 --> 01:08:36.153
scientists about what I'm doing.

01:08:37.487 --> 01:08:39.960
So to really have influence
in the broader world.

01:08:39.960 --> 01:08:42.063
But you kind of want to confront,

01:08:43.320 --> 01:08:47.190
you need to talk to other
types of social scientists

01:08:47.190 --> 01:08:49.233
and I think that's not rewarded so much.

01:08:50.850 --> 01:08:52.800
- So I'm getting the flag

01:08:52.800 --> 01:08:54.120
that we're at the end of our time.

01:08:54.120 --> 01:08:55.990
But I just wanted to flag that

01:08:56.892 --> 01:08:59.890
that this particular
interaction is actually

01:09:01.170 --> 01:09:04.990
an environmental social feedback loop

01:09:06.151 --> 01:09:07.960
that we don't know that much about

01:09:08.875 --> 01:09:10.620
and sort of proves the point of the panel.

01:09:10.620 --> 01:09:14.040
How do we tell better structured,

01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:19.040
informative stories about
how to amplify exactly

01:09:19.080 --> 01:09:22.740
this loop between seeing the change,

01:09:22.740 --> 01:09:25.950
being more excited about it,
having more conversations.

01:09:25.950 --> 01:09:28.230
This isn't humans and
climate models and climate

01:09:28.230 --> 01:09:31.500
and human models, but this
is the kind of conversation

01:09:31.500 --> 01:09:34.560
that we need to figure
out how to represent,

01:09:34.560 --> 01:09:36.723
understand, and amplify.

01:09:37.776 --> 01:09:39.540
So we'll leave it on that optimistic note

01:09:40.682 --> 01:09:42.930
and that to-do list for all of us

01:09:42.930 --> 01:09:45.900
and head into the reception and dinner.

01:09:45.900 --> 01:09:47.487
The panel will be around.

01:09:47.487 --> 01:09:49.890
And Tejal, thank you
so much for joining us.

01:09:49.890 --> 01:09:52.830
I hope you do get some sleep
and we'll be following up.

01:09:52.830 --> 01:09:55.347
If you have questions for
Tejal, I think just let me know

01:09:55.347 --> 01:09:57.630
and we can convey them to Tejal

01:09:57.630 --> 01:10:00.900
and hopefully build
something from this panel

01:10:00.900 --> 01:10:03.000
to amplify the interaction.

01:10:03.000 --> 01:10:04.166
Thank you.

01:10:04.166 --> 01:10:06.999
(people clapping)

01:10:16.909 --> 01:10:19.326
(soft music)

